	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: Performance was extracted for the 1-year random forest model using all preoperative and postoperative variables, evaluated across the outer hold-out folds of nested five-fold cross-validation. The model achieved AUC 0.86 (95% CI 0.80–0.92) and Brier score 0.10. Accuracy, PPV, sensitivity, specificity, NPV, and F1-score were not reported. The preoperative-only 1-year model achieved AUC 0.67 (95% CI 0.56–0.78; Brier score 0.15). Formal calibration plots, calibration intercept or slope, and DCA were not reported.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study included the institutional cohort of 581 adolescent and adult patients who underwent OHT during a clearly specified period, and mortality status and date of death were obtained for all patients from Hungary’s National Health Insurance Database. No major selective exclusions were reported.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The primary high-performing models included postoperative maximum or minimum laboratory values, ICU and ventilation duration, total transfusions, postoperative complications, and duration of circulatory support. A fixed prediction time for these variables was not defined, and some predictors may occur after substantial follow-up or be directly affected by impending death, creating temporal leakage and reverse-causation risk.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The outcomes were objectively defined 30-day and 1-year all-cause mortality. Vital status and date of death were obtained for all patients through the national insurance database, supporting consistent and complete outcome ascertainment.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Nested cross-validation, fold-specific preprocessing, and training-only SMOTE were important strengths. However, performance was internally validated only, there was no external or temporal validation, the cohort contained relatively few deaths, and the all-feature models incorporated postoperative variables with unclear availability at the intended prediction time. These issues may produce optimistic performance despite the rigorous cross-validation pipeline.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates explainable machine-learning models for 30-day and 1-year mortality after heart transplantation using recipient, donor, and perioperative data. It is directly relevant to AI prediction research in thoracic organ transplantation, although the all-feature model is applicable only after postoperative information has accrued.

